[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Server-Sent Events (SSE): Receiving Real-time Updates from the Server"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Server-Sent Events (SSE) is a technology that enables a server to push real-time updates to a client's web browser over a single HTTP connection. Unlike WebSockets, SSE is unidirectional, meaning the server sends data to the client, but the client does not send data back to the server over the same connection. This makes SSE a simpler and more efficient solution for applications that primarily require server-to-client communication, such as live news feeds, stock tickers, and social media updates."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Basics of SSE"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"SSE relies on the `EventSource` interface in HTML5. The client-side JavaScript code creates an `EventSource` object, specifying the URL of the server-side endpoint that will be streaming the updates. The server responds with a MIME type of `text/event-stream` and then sends data in a specific format."},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"The data format for SSE is simple. Each data field is preceded by `data:` and terminated by a newline character (`\n`). Multiple data fields can be sent in a single event, and each event is separated by two newline characters (`\n\n`). Other optional fields include `event:` (to specify an event type) and `id:` (to assign an ID to the event)."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Client-Side Implementation (JavaScript)"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The client-side implementation involves creating an `EventSource` object and attaching event listeners to handle incoming events. Here's a basic example:"},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"const eventSource = new EventSource('/events');

eventSource.onopen = () => {
  console.log('SSE connection opened');
};

eventSource.onmessage = (event) => {
  console.log('Received event:', event.data);
  // Update the UI with the received data
  document.getElementById('data-container').textContent = event.data;
};

eventSource.onerror = (error) => {
  console.error('SSE error:', error);
  eventSource.close(); // Close the connection on error
};

// Optional: Listen for specific event types
eventSource.addEventListener('update', (event) => {
  console.log('Received update event:', event.data);
  // Handle the 'update' event
});"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example:

*   `new EventSource('/events')` creates a new `EventSource` object, connecting to the `/events` endpoint on the server.
*   `onopen` is called when the connection is successfully established.
*   `onmessage` is called when a generic event is received from the server. The `event.data` property contains the data sent by the server.
*   `onerror` is called if an error occurs during the connection.
*   `addEventListener('update', ...)` demonstrates how to listen for specific event types sent by the server using the `event:` field."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Server-Side Implementation (Node.js Example)"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here's a simple Node.js example using Express to implement an SSE endpoint:"},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"const express = require('express');
const app = express();
const port = 3000;

app.get('/events', (req, res) => {
  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/event-stream');
  res.setHeader('Cache-Control', 'no-cache');
  res.setHeader('Connection', 'keep-alive');
  res.flushHeaders();

  let counter = 0;
  const intervalId = setInterval(() => {
    const data = `Hello from server! Counter: ${counter++}`;
    res.write(`data: ${data}\n\n`);
  }, 1000);

  req.on('close', () => {
    clearInterval(intervalId);
    console.log('Client disconnected');
    res.end();
  });
});

app.listen(port, () => {
  console.log(`SSE server listening at http://localhost:${port}`);
});"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example:

*   The `/events` endpoint sets the appropriate headers: `Content-Type: text/event-stream`, `Cache-Control: no-cache`, and `Connection: keep-alive`.
*   `res.flushHeaders()` sends the headers to the client.
*   `setInterval` sends a new event every second with an incrementing counter.
*   `req.on('close', ...)` handles client disconnections, clearing the interval to prevent memory leaks."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Sending Specific Event Types"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can specify event types using the `event:` field. This allows the client to listen for specific events using `addEventListener`."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"res.write(`event: update\n`);
res.write(`data: This is an update event\n\n`);"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"On the client-side, you would listen for this event like this:"},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"eventSource.addEventListener('update', (event) => {
  console.log('Received update event:', event.data);
  // Handle the 'update' event
});"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Sending Event IDs"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `id:` field can be used to assign a unique ID to each event. The browser automatically tracks the last event ID received. If the connection is lost and re-established, the browser sends a `Last-Event-ID` header with the last known ID, allowing the server to resume sending events from where it left off. This is useful for ensuring that no events are missed during temporary disconnections."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"let eventId = 0;

setInterval(() => {
  const data = `Event with ID: ${eventId}`;
  res.write(`id: ${eventId++}\n`);
  res.write(`data: ${data}\n\n`);
}, 1000);"},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Error Handling and Reconnection"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `EventSource` object automatically attempts to reconnect if the connection is lost. You can control the reconnection delay by setting the `retry` field on the server-side. The value is in milliseconds."},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"res.write(`retry: 5000\n`); // Retry every 5 seconds"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"On the client-side, you should also implement error handling to gracefully handle connection errors and provide feedback to the user."},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Advantages of SSE"},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Simplicity: SSE is easier to implement than WebSockets, especially for server-to-client communication."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Standard Protocol: SSE uses the standard HTTP protocol, making it compatible with existing infrastructure and easier to debug."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Automatic Reconnection: The `EventSource` object automatically handles reconnection, reducing the amount of code you need to write."},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Disadvantages of SSE"},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Unidirectional: SSE is only suitable for server-to-client communication. For bidirectional communication, WebSockets are a better choice."},{"order":32,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Limited Browser Support: While SSE is widely supported, older browsers may not support it. You may need to use a polyfill for older browsers."},{"order":33,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"Server-Sent Events provide a simple and efficient way to push real-time updates from the server to the client. By understanding the basics of the `EventSource` interface and the SSE data format, you can easily implement SSE in your web applications to provide a better user experience."}]
